WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.589
In Sri Lanka, a tropical island nation just
a few miles southeast of India, children never

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:09.050
get a snow day.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:14.160
Neither do they have to contend with earthquakes,
volcanoes, or sandstorms.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.769
But three million of its students now face
a different kind of disaster: their schools

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:24.529
can’t afford ink — or even paper on which
to print it.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:29.119
On March 19th, the education department of
its largest province announced the cancellation

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:34.360
of all exams for grades 9, 10, and 11 for
that exact reason.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:41.290
Today, however, paper is the least of its
concerns, as entire cities grind, as we speak,

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.410
to a dangerous and unprecedented halt.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.570
Prices for essential goods — everything
from rice to fuel to medicine — have doubled,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.470
then tripled, and continued rising.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:59.510
That, combined with power outages lasting
up to 15 hours a day, have Christian nuns,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.720
Hindus, Muslims, and Buddhist monks, who just
10 years ago fought each other in a Civil

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.900
War, all joining hands on the streets in protest.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.610
Unfortunately, the worst is likely yet to
come.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:19.250
As the last remaining supplies dwindle away,
the current 9-hours of electricity and severely

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.560
overpriced groceries may soon be remembered
as the “good days”.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.340
And yet, Sri Lanka, experts warn, is just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:36.860
Though unique as a country, the roots of many
of its current problems are far too common.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.870
The United Nations warned last month of a
“perfect storm” brewing in the developing

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:42.920
world.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.060
One expected to leave, quote, “deep and
long-lasting scars”.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.950
Sri Lanka, in other words, may simply be the
first domino to fall this year.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.450
And understanding how it got here — unable
to afford even paper — may offer a preview

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.659
of what comes next.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:08.270
Sponsored by Storyblocks, where you can find
over a million high-quality videos for your

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:14.350
creative projects, business, or, like us,
YouTube channel.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.860
This is an imaginary country.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.090
If it imports more “things” than it exports,
we say it has a “trade deficit”.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:27.220
Conversely, if it exports more than it imports,
it has a “trade surplus”.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:32.099
Now, because our frame of reference is personal
finance, in which you’re told to spend less

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:37.520
than you save, or corporations, where “profit”
sounds a lot like a “surplus” of money,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.650
it’s tempting to see deficits as a bad thing.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.380
Of course, as you may already know, it’s
much more complicated.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.240
The United States, for instance, has a huge
deficit.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:53.400
The slower-growing Japanese economy, meanwhile,
has a giant surplus.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.670
But what’s all too easy to forget is that
the U.S., and, to a lesser extent, Japan,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.180
are weird countries.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.690
China may “win” the trade war with the
U.S., for example, but what does it do with

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.380
all that money?

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.340
Invest in the United States!

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.930
But, remember, this is a very unique set of
circumstances.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:20.390
It’s what economists mean when they say
the U.S. has a “privileged position”.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:26.730
When China sells things to America, it wants
to buy, with that money, U.S. dollars, not

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.010
Somali shillings or Iraqi Dinars!

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.340
…Or Sri Lankan rupees.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.440
To see why this is an issue for a country
like Sri Lanka, stop thinking in terms of

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.440
imports versus exports and, for the sake of
simplicity, think instead about the actual,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.629
physical supply of Dollars.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:54.599
When it buys, say, milk, from next door India,
it generally pays for those goods with U.S.

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:57.379
Dollars because that’s what India wants.

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:04.500
Likewise, when Sri Lanka sells its tea to
the U.K., the British pay it in Dollars.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.750
You should start noticing two potential problems
here.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:14.659
First, if a country imports more than it exports,
you can see how it could just run out of USD

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:18.859
— it would be effectively spending more
than it’s saving.

00:04:18.859 --> 00:04:25.100
And second, running out of USD is problematic
because it’s what you need to buy imports.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.710
In other words, for countries not named “the
United States of America”, this balance

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.750
of payments really is important.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:38.490
Here, where a negative value equals a trade
deficit, you can see that Sri Lanka has been

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.360
“in the red”, so to speak, for quite some
time.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:48.409
It imports a huge amount of rice, wheat, sugar,
and medicine — not to mention all its oil.

00:04:48.409 --> 00:04:54.699
Now, all this might sound like trade deficits
are some horrible, no-good, very bad thing.

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:58.949
But, as you can see here, most countries run
deficits.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:04.860
There’s nothing rare or necessarily catastrophic
about having a negative balance of trade on

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.469
its own.

00:05:06.469 --> 00:05:11.509
What all these countries do to make up the
difference is simply borrow.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:16.500
If that sounds unsustainable, well, yes and
no.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:22.110
In the case of Sri Lanka, its government has
a long history of gross mismanagement.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:26.539
Just last year, for example, the government
banned the import of all chemical fertilizers

00:05:26.539 --> 00:05:30.689
in a bid to become the world’s first “all-organic”
nation.

00:05:30.689 --> 00:05:36.869
To the surprise of no one but the president,
crop yields were immediately cut in half.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:41.300
You could make a pretty good case that Sri
Lanka was destined for a crisis of some kind

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.669
and that it was only a matter of when.

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:49.520
Yet, just a few short years ago, the country
showed real economic promise.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.509
It had other ways of getting its hands on
Dollars.

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:59.389
In 2019 tourism accounted for 22% of its total
GDP.

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:04.849
Lonely Planet ranked the country the number
one best place to visit that same year.

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:12.120
There are beautiful beaches, wild leopards,
yoga retreats, and stunning natural landscapes.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.250
Another source of foreign exchange was remittances.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:21.710
Workers would go overseas, earn foreign currency,
and send it to their families back home.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:26.340
But… you can probably guess what happened
in 2020.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.240
Tourism effectively fell to zero overnight.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.680
Actually, the decline in tourists began even
before the pandemic.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.569
In the Spring of 2019, terrorists bombed a
string of churches and hotels across the capital,

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:41.619
Colombo.

00:06:41.619 --> 00:06:47.889
The tragic and shocking death of 269 people
— at least 45 of which were foreign nationals

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:52.650
— led to a 70% drop in tourist arrivals.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:07:01.430
Its $7.9 billion of foreign reserves in 2019
fell to just $1.6 billion by 2021.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:06.669
And if that sounds bad, there's an extra reason,
on top of the normal badness, that makes it

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:07.669
even worse.

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:13.580
You may have heard that some countries intentionally
under or over-value their currencies.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.899
Sri Lanka is one of those places.

00:07:16.899 --> 00:07:23.460
Let’s say a bag of rice from India costs
one USD to import, and one USD is currently

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.960
worth 200 Sri Lankan rupees.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:33.470
If Sri Lanka can convince everyone that, “no,
actually 150 rupees is the value of a U.S.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:38.669
Dollar”, well now that rice is effectively
25% cheaper.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:43.330
The Central Bank, in other words, overvalued
the rupee because the country depends so heavily

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:44.550
on imports.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:50.259
By keeping staple goods affordable, authorities
in developing nations maintain economic, and

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:53.029
therefore, political stability.

00:07:53.029 --> 00:07:57.629
If you’re wondering why all countries don’t
do this, the answer is what Sri Lanka is going

00:07:57.629 --> 00:07:59.789
through now.

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:04.870
Actually maintaining that artificial exchange
rate involves buying and selling — or borrowing

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:10.360
— foreign currency, which, remember, Sri
Lanka is running out of.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:15.139
Here you can see where the central bank kept
the rupee at roughly the same value until

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:20.139
it suddenly couldn’t, when it's real, market
value took over.

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:27.139
The government, meanwhile, was still pretending
that 1 USD was worth, for example, 150 rupees,

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:30.639
when everyone else had decided it was worth
200.

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:37.310
Here’s what that means in practice: Walk
into a bank, hand them one USD and they’ll

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.850
give you back 150 rupees.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:45.010
Walk, on the other hand, into a private shop,
hand them that same dollar bill, and they’ll

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:46.720
give you, say, 200.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.120
It doesn’t take long before word gets out:
don’t deposit your foreign currency in the

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.380
bank — “it’s a scam”.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:09:00.180
Migrant workers quickly learned not to send
their remittances home through official channels.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.690
Because of this, the central bank has even
less foreign currency, making it even harder

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:07.800
to maintain its fake exchange rate.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.000
And this is when things get really ugly, really
fast.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.260
What Sri Lanka is experiencing now are the
effects of three, interconnected problems.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:22.630
First, because the country is running out
of Dollars, it can’t buy enough imports

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:27.460
to keep up with demand — meaning there just
isn’t enough rice, paper, or fuel to go

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:28.470
around.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:34.270
Second, at the same time, people’s savings
are suddenly worth far less today than they

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.930
were yesterday.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:39.630
Years of hard work — gone overnight.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:45.270
And third, not only is the Rupee rapidly depreciating
in value, but no one knows where, exactly,

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:46.321
the bottom is.

00:09:46.321 --> 00:09:51.270
It doesn’t take a rocket scientist to see
the trend.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.610
Last week I could afford a dozen bananas.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:56.490
Today, half as many.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:10:02.400
Every rational household starts stocking up
on essential goods — leading to chaos, confusion,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.760
and chronic undersupply.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:11.190
Lines at gas stations were so long that two
men collapsed and died waiting.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:17.690
Needless to say, the pandemic plus poor governance
had placed the country on tenuous ground already.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:20.430
Then the war in Ukraine began.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.820
The conflict and the subsequent sanctions
are currently causing the largest commodity

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.990
shock in half a century.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:32.120
Here you can see, for instance, the price
— and just as importantly, its volatility

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:40.050
— of wheat and maize, which Russia and Ukraine
produce 28 and 15% of the global supply of,

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.250
respectively.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:46.840
Ukrainian exports alone previously fed 400
million people.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:51.720
Some countries get nearly all their wheat
from one or both.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.940
These are just the places which source at
least 80% of their wheat from Russia and Ukraine.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:04.660
On top of this, Russia also supplies 15% of
the world’s fertilizer, and, just as importantly,

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.650
the fuel used to make fertilizer.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:11.720
If you drive a car and live on planet Earth,
you probably don’t need to be told that

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.000
oil prices are off the charts.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.170
Many countries have or will react by banning
food exports — further exacerbating the

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:24.070
situation for net importers like Sri Lanka.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:29.940
Now, like trade deficits, developing countries
being unable to pay back their loans is not

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.260
all that uncommon.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:36.370
There’s a system for this sort of thing
— the equivalent of “bankruptcy” for

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:37.370
nations.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:42.140
Normally, organizations like the IMF and World
Bank step in.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:49.260
Sri Lanka last received a bail-out from the
IMF for $1.6 billion in 2016.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:51.540
But this time is different.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:57.800
Bail-outs, you see, are conditional on things
like having a functional government and implementing

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:01.450
economic policies which are often unpopular.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.010
The IMF doesn’t just write checks on a whim
— it wants to know that the country won’t

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:09.710
end up in the same situation 6 months later.

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:14.120
These are conditions Sri Lanka simply can’t
meet.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.000
While citizens are very justifiably angry
at their government, the unfortunate truth

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:25.160
is that political turmoil and social unrest
will likely only discourage foreign assistance.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:30.320
Protestors are right that the country’s
economic woes didn’t come from nowhere.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.730
The groundwork for the current crisis can
be traced back decades — when Sri Lanka

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:40.330
began paying off old loans with new ones,
in what the World Bank has compared to a Pyramid

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:41.880
scheme.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:46.420
While it’s easy to connect the dots between
an unprecedented global pandemic and a sudden

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:53.160
economic collapse, so too is it easy to blame
Chinese loans, but neither theory captures

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.390
the whole truth.

00:12:55.390 --> 00:13:00.530
Only about 10% of Sri Lanka’s debt is owned
by China, and its leaders have been pursuing

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:05.380
disastrous policies for years before any of
the recent events.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:11.410
In 2019, it cut taxes precisely when it needed
public revenue the most.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:15.940
Poor governance is not the only but it is
an important ingredient to the cocktail of

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.490
calamities it’s dealing with now.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:25.570
What’s surprising is not Sri Lanka’s poor
leadership, but how quickly it all unraveled.

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:31.140
In a matter of weeks, millions of Sri Lankans
went from living a fairly middle-class lifestyle

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:37.790
— cars, college education, and leisure — to
wondering whether they can eat dinner.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.580
This is how countries spiral out of control.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:47.620
A shock, or, in this case, a trifecta of shocks,
exposes a nation’s weak governance.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:52.360
From there, there’s no limit to how bad
things can get.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:57.260
For which other country or countries will
the confluence of the pandemic, financial

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:03.820
tightening, and a drastic increase in commodity
prices soon expose a weak foundation?

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:08.410
When the Fed raises interest rates, as it’s
doing now, developing countries have a harder

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:11.210
time paying off their loans.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:15.980
This requires that they choose between servicing
their debt or spending what little money they

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.360
have saving lives at home.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.210
Some experts are pointing to Pakistan — whose
currency is currently in rapid decline — as

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:26.660
a possible next domino to fall.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:31.279
This domino, however, is nuclear.

00:14:31.279 --> 00:14:37.300
The war alone is expected to decrease global
GDP growth by 1% this year.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.430
Put simply: a global pandemic, a change in
interest rates, and the largest armed conflict

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:48.080
in Europe since the Cold War, would each,
by themselves, be hugely significant.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:50.250
But all three at the same time?

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:54.910
That’s why the UN calls this a “perfect
storm”.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:15:01.090
Here you can see the last 15 years of agriculture,
commodity, and grain prices.

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:05.520
While high prices don’t guarantee social
unrest, they tend to correlate.

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:09.380
This, for example, is the Arab Spring.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:15.100
This, meanwhile, is where prices were before
the war in Ukraine.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:18.050
This is where they’re at now.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:23.529
What for the rich world will be an inconvenience
— high gas prices, inefficient supply chains,

00:15:23.529 --> 00:15:29.210
and, possibly, even recession, will, for the
developing world, mean the undoing of decades

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:34.000
worth of progress in healthcare, education,
and general prosperity.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:39.050
Now, you may be wondering why Sri Lanka has
been the focus of this video, when this is

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:41.430
really a global issue.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:47.400
The reason is simple: What’s happening in
Sri Lanka is tragic, visible, and all too

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:53.620
real — which makes it a useful way of humanizing
what are, ultimately, numbers on a graph.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.770
And that’s why I love Storyblocks.

